  <section|The theorem><label|the-theorem>

  Let <math|G\<subset\>U<around|(|m|)>> be a closed subgroup whose Lie
  algebra <math|\<frak-g\>> is simple and nonabelian, for instance
  <math|S*U<around|(|N|)>> with <math|N\<geqslant\>2>; <math|G> need not be
  connected. The main theorem is <verbatim|YangMills.jointExistence>; its
  precise statement is in <hlink|statement.md|statement.tm>. It asserts that
  there is one family of Wilson lattice gauge theories for <math|G> on
  four-dimensional tori, consisting of:

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>spacings <math|a<rsub|N>=1/D<rsub|N>*\<downarrow\>*0> and physical
    side lengths <math|L<rsub|p>*\<uparrow\>*\<infty\>>;

    <item>bare couplings <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p>> with
    <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p>/log <around|(|1/a<rsub|N>|)>\<to\>b<rsub|\<ast\>>\<gtr\>0>
    uniformly in <math|p>;

    <item>insertions <math|I<rsub|N,p><around|(|v,y|)>> indexed by tokens
    <math|v>, each a continuous gauge-invariant function of the links in a
    stencil of fixed size in lattice units around the site <math|y>.
  </itemize-minus>

  The family has the following property. For every strictly increasing
  sequence <math|p<around|(|k|)>> of volume indices there are a further
  subsequence <math|p<around|(|q<around|(|k|)>|)>> and a continuum hierarchy
  satisfying (1)\U(5) below.

  <strong|(1) Lattice origin.> Let <math|<math-up|Conf><rsub|n>\<subset\><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>|)><rsup|n>>
  be the open set of configurations of distinct points. For every <math|n>,
  every list of tokens <math|v> and every
  <math|f\<in\>C<rsub|c><rsup|\<infty\>><around|(|<math-up|Conf><rsub|n>|)>>,

  <\equation*>
    lim<rsub|k\<to\>\<infty\>> <space|0.17em>lim<rsub|N\<to\>\<infty\>>
    <space|0.17em>a<rsub|N><rsup|4*n>*<big|sum><rsub|y>f<around|(|<wide|y|^>|)><space|0.17em><around*|\<langle\>|<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>I<rsub|N,p<around|(|q<around|(|k|)>|)>><around|(|v<rsub|i>,y<rsub|i>|)>|\<rangle\>><rsub|N,p<around|(|q<around|(|k|)>|)>>=<big|int>f<around|(|x|)>*<space|0.17em>S<rsup|<math-up|mix>><around|(|\<pi\>*v;x|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x.
  </equation*>

  Here the sum runs over <math|n>-tuples of sites, <math|<wide|y|^>> are
  their physical positions, and <math|<around|\<langle\>|\<cdummy\>|\<rangle\>><rsub|N,p>>
  is the Wilson measure.

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>The inner limit runs over the whole sequence of spacings, at every
    volume whose fundamental box contains the support of <math|f>; this holds
    for all large <math|k>.

    <item>The test functions are joint functions of the <math|n> points and
    vanish near all coincidences.

    <item>One subsequence serves all <math|n>, <math|v> and <math|f>.
  </itemize-minus>

  <strong|(2) Phases.> On <math|<math-up|Conf><rsub|n>>,
  <math|S<rsup|<math-up|mix>>=<big|int><rsub|X>S<rsup|\<xi\>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><around|(|\<xi\>|)>>.
  Here <math|X> is a compact metric space, <math|\<nu\>> is a probability
  measure of full support, and <math|\<xi\>\<mapsto\>S<rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|a;x|)>>
  is continuous. All correlations <math|S<rsup|s>>,
  <math|s\<in\>X\<sqcup\><around|{|<math-up|mix>|}>>, are smooth and
  permutation symmetric on <math|<math-up|Conf><rsub|n>>.

  <strong|(3) Reconstruction.> A selected phase <math|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>>
  carries a Wightman theory:

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>a strongly continuous unitary representation of the proper
    orthochronous Poincaré group;

    <item>spectrum in the closed forward cone;

    <item>a vacuum that is unique up to scalars among translation-invariant
    vectors;

    <item>fields that are operator-valued tempered distributions on the
    invariant domain spanned by field monomials applied to the vacuum;

    <item>local commutativity and faithful field labels.
  </itemize-minus>

  Its Wightman functions are boundary values, on product test functions, of
  the continuation of <math|S<rsup|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>>> that is holomorphic
  in the ordered complex times.

  <strong|(4) Operator product expansion.> One system of coefficients, with
  finitely many local fields of each degree, satisfies finite-order OPE
  bounds in the mixed state and in every phase.

  <strong|(5) Nonfreeness.> Let <math|e<rsub|4>> be the time direction. A
  field <math|\<Theta\>> of degree 4 with vanishing vacuum expectation
  satisfies

  <\equation*>
    lim<rsub|r*\<downarrow\>*0> <space|0.17em>r<rsup|8>*log<rsup|2><around|(|1/r|)>*<space|0.17em>G<rsub|\<Theta\>><around|(|r|)>=<frac|3*dim<rsub|\<bbb-R\>>
    \<frak-g\>|\<pi\><rsup|4>>,<space|2em>G<rsub|\<Theta\>><around|(|r|)>=S<rsup|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>>*<around*|(|\<Theta\>*<around|(|-<tfrac|r|2>e<rsub|4>|)>*<space|0.17em>\<Theta\><around|(|<tfrac|r|2>e<rsub|4>|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Moreover, <math|G<rsub|\<Theta\>>> agrees on no interval
  <math|<around|(|0,\<delta\>|)>> with a finite Wick profile, that is, a
  finite sum of products of free mass-shell kernels
  <math|<big|int>E<rsup|q>*h<around|(|\<b-up-p\>|)>*<around|(|2*E|)><rsup|-1>*e<rsup|-r*E>*<space|0.17em>d<rsup|3>*\<b-up-p\>>.

  The lattice data are fixed before the volume sequence. The refinement and
  all continuum objects may depend on that sequence.

  <strong|The stronger statement.> The theorem is a projection of a longer
  statement, <verbatim|YangMills.constructed>. That statement holds for every
  value <math|b\<gtr\>0> of the penalty parameter of the block-spin kernel;
  the public theorem takes <math|b=1>. The construction supplies the
  following facts; the slope value is established in the proof of regularity,
  rather than stated as a separate conjunct of
  <verbatim|YangMills.constructed>:

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>The slope is <math|b<rsub|\<ast\>>=\<kappa\><rsub|G>=11*C<rsub|\<frak-g\>>/<around|(|24*\<pi\><rsup|2>|)>>.

    <item>A token is a pair <math|<around|(|d,c|)>>, with <math|c> in the
    space <math|V<rsub|d>> of gauge-invariant polynomials of weight at most
    <math|d> in the curvature and its covariant derivatives (curvature has
    weight 2, and each derivative adds 1).

    <item>The field spaces are <math|F<rsub|d>=V<rsub|d>/N<rsub|d>>, where
    <math|N<rsub|d>> consists of the labels whose mixed correlations with all
    separated spectators vanish. The label space of the relativistic theory
    is the directed union of the <math|F<rsub|d>>, so there is no finite
    field multiplet.

    <item><math|\<Theta\>> is the centred trace of a constructed conserved
    stress tensor.

    <item>Every phase carries an irreducible Wightman theory, with low-energy
    bounds that are uniform in the phase.

    <item>The correlations extend to distributions across coincident points;
    uniqueness is not asserted.

    <item>After finite-dimensional changes of frame, the rescaled
    correlations converge at short distance to those of <math|dim \<frak-g\>>
    free Maxwell fields.

    <item>Nested and integrated forms of the OPE hold, as do further
    nonfreeness tests.
  </itemize-minus>

  This overview describes the proof of the stronger statement.

  <strong|What is not claimed.>

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>No mass gap is proved; the available spectral bounds in every phase
    are polynomial.

    <item>The infinite-volume limit is not shown to be unique or independent
    of the subsequence.

    <item>The phases are not shown to agree, and <math|X> is not shown to be
    a point.

    <item>The mixed state is not shown to cluster.

    <item>The limit is not shown to be independent of the lattice data.

    <item>The two limits are taken in a fixed order. No diagonal limit,
    exchange of limits or rate of convergence is asserted.

    <item>No phase is shown to be a limit of lattice states; the lattice
    limit is the mixture.

    <item>The public theorem asserts nothing at coincident points and no
    Euclidean axioms. The construction proves those axioms internally.

    <item>There is no continuum measure on connections, no Wilson-loop
    observable or area law, and no particle or scattering theory.

    <item>Only finite Wick profiles are excluded. Generalized free fields
    with a continuous mass distribution, infinite sums and general composites
    of Gaussian fields are not excluded.

    <item>The OPE is a finite-order bound and is not compared with
    perturbation theory.
  </itemize-minus>

  <strong|Normalizations.> On <math|\<frak-g\>>, put
  <math|<around|\<langle\>|X,Y|\<rangle\>><rsub|\<frak-g\>>=-<tfrac|1|m><math-up|Re><space|0.17em><math-up|tr><around|(|X*Y|)>>,
  and define <math|C<rsub|\<frak-g\>>\<gtr\>0> by
  <math|<math-up|tr><around|(|<math-up|ad>X<space|0.17em><math-up|ad>Y|)>=-C<rsub|\<frak-g\>><around|\<langle\>|X,Y|\<rangle\>><rsub|\<frak-g\>>>.
  Two facts fix the coupling convention:

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item><math|1-<tfrac|1|m><math-up|Re><space|0.17em><math-up|tr><space|0.17em>e<rsup|X>=<tfrac|1|2>\|X\|<rsub|\<frak-g\>><rsup|2>+O<around|(|<around|\||X|\|><rsup|4>|)>>;

    <item>each unoriented plaquette is counted once in the Wilson action.
  </itemize-minus>

  Hence <math|\<beta\>=1/g<rsub|0><rsup|2>> for the continuum action
  <math|<frac|1|2*g<rsub|0><rsup|2>>*<big|int><big|sum><rsub|\<mu\>\<less\>\<nu\>><around|\||F<rsub|\<mu\>*\<nu\>>|\|><rsup|2><rsub|\<frak-g\>>>.
  The constructed couplings satisfy, uniformly in <math|p>,

  <\equation*>
    <frac|\<beta\><rsub|N,p>|log <around|(|1/a<rsub|N>|)>>\<longrightarrow\>\<kappa\><rsub|G>=<frac|11*C<rsub|\<frak-g\>>|24*\<pi\><rsup|2>>=2*b<rsub|0>,<space|2em>b<rsub|0>=<frac|11*C<rsub|\<frak-g\>>|48*\<pi\><rsup|2>>.
  </equation*>

  For <math|S*U<around|(|N|)>> in the defining representation,
  <math|C<rsub|\<frak-g\>>=2*N<rsup|2>> and
  <math|\<kappa\><rsub|G>=11*N<rsup|2>/<around|(|12*\<pi\><rsup|2>|)>>. In
  that case <math|\<beta\>> is the usual Wilson coupling
  <math|2*N/g<rsup|2>>, and the law above is the one-loop running
  <math|1/g<rsup|2>\<simeq\><around|(|11*N/24*\<pi\><rsup|2>|)>*log
  <around|(|1/a|)>> of Gross\UWilczek and Politzer.

  <section|The strategy><label|the-strategy>

  <strong|Order of limits.> One lattice family is fixed first. Correlations
  of renormalized insertions are taken to the limit <math|a<rsub|N>\<to\>0>
  at fixed volume, along the whole sequence of spacings. The volume then
  tends to infinity along a subsequence of the prescribed one. The
  renormalization group (RG) is used only for the first limit. Its levels run
  from the Wilson lattice (level <math|N>) to the torus of side
  <math|L<rsub|p>> at unit spacing (level <math|0>). The bare coupling is
  tuned so that the effective coupling at level <math|0> is one fixed number
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>>, the same for all <math|N> and <math|p>. No
  RG step is taken above the unit scale; infinite volume is reached by
  compactness, from local bounds that are uniform in the volume.

  <strong|Ultraviolet construction (Parts I\UIII).> At each level the density
  is written as two pieces:

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>an explicit gauge-invariant action, whose normalized
    curvature-squared coefficient is the running coupling
    <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,m>>;

    <item>polymer activities: regular ones, controlled where the curvature is
    small, and forced ones, which carry the large-field regions.
  </itemize-minus>

  One block-spin step integrates the fine links against an exactly
  normalized, gauge-covariant probability kernel:

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>In the small-field region this is a Gaussian integral around a
    stationary background field, organized by a cluster expansion.

    <item>Large-field regions are kept and paid for by a lower bound on the
    quadratic energy of the step.

    <item>The marginal term is absorbed into the coupling. The coupling
    changes by the one-loop amount <math|\<kappa\><rsub|G>*log L<rsub|m>> up
    to an error, and block ratios that grow slowly along the levels make the
    accumulated error <math|o<around|(|log <around|(|1/a<rsub|m>|)>|)>>.
  </itemize-minus>

  The same steps transport inserted observables. This second layer produces
  what later parts use:

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>chessboard bounds on renormalized insertions and on their
    difference quotients, uniform in the volume;

    <item>convergence at fixed volume, obtained from fixed-level convergence
    and uniform observable tails. The construction also supplies analytic
    estimates in a tuning parameter.
  </itemize-minus>

  <strong|Euclidean limit (Part IV).>

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>Chessboard bounds make the correlations locally bounded, uniformly
    in the volume.

    <item>A transfer-matrix representation and complex interpolation make
    them equicontinuous, and Arzelà\UAscoli gives the subsequence.

    <item>Difference quotients of insertions are close to insertions of
    higher degree. This yields derivative maps on the field spaces and
    kernels that are smooth on <math|<math-up|Conf><rsub|n>>.

    <item>The limit inherits translations, the signed permutations of the
    axes and reflection positivity. It does not inherit continuous rotations
    or clustering.
  </itemize-minus>

  <strong|Symmetry (Part VI).>

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>Lattice integration by parts gives shell identities for a conserved
    tensor. Its normalization is fixed by short-distance comparison with free
    Maxwell theory.

    <item>Reflection positivity controls the flux at infinity, and
    analyticity extends the Ward identities to all separated configurations.

    <item>The infinitesimal rotations integrate to an <math|O<around|(|4|)>>
    action, again by comparison with free Maxwell theory.
  </itemize-minus>

  <strong|Phases and reconstruction (Part V).>

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>The zero-energy subspace of the Osterwalder\USchrader (OS) space of
    the mixed state carries a commutative <math|C<rsup|\<ast\>>>-algebra; its
    proof of commutativity uses the <math|O<around|(|4|)>> action. The
    characters of this algebra are the phases.

    <item>Each phase is reconstructed as a Wightman theory, from bounds on
    operators inserted in thin time slabs. Lorentz covariance comes from the
    Ward identities.

    <item>A low-energy spectral bound, uniform in the phase and derived from
    locality, upgrades vacuum uniqueness from almost every phase to every
    phase.
  </itemize-minus>

  <strong|Identification (Part VII).>

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>Along sequences of scales tending to zero, the theory converges,
    after changes of frame, to free Maxwell theory.

    <item>The one-loop coefficient produces an inverse-logarithmic law for
    the centered stress-tensor trace two-point function, and that law
    excludes the specified finite Wick profiles.

    <item>The RG also yields the OPE.
  </itemize-minus>

  The logical order is not the order of the parts. Part VI uses the OS spaces
  of the mixed state from Part V and the free comparison from Part VII. Part
  V uses the <math|O<around|(|4|)>> action from Part VI. Part VIII assembles
  everything for one family and one limit table.

  <section|Part I. The lattice theory><label|part-i-the-lattice-theory>

  <em|Unit: <hlink|I.1 \V Lattice, symmetries, gauge groups and the Wilson
  measure|outline/units/I-1.tm>.>

  On <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|\<ell\>>=<around|(|\<bbb-Z\>/\<ell\>*\<bbb-Z\>|)><rsup|4>>,
  a configuration assigns <math|U<around|(|y,\<mu\>|)>\<in\>G> to each
  positively oriented link. With <math|h> the normalized Haar measure, the
  Wilson measure is

  <\equation*>
    d*\<mu\><rsub|\<ell\>,\<beta\>><around|(|U|)>=Z<rsup|-1>*exp
    <around*|[|-\<beta\>*<big|sum><rsub|y><big|sum><rsub|\<mu\>\<less\>\<nu\>><around*|(|1-<tfrac|1|m><math-up|Re><space|0.17em><math-up|tr><space|0.17em>U<rsub|y;\<mu\>*\<nu\>>|)>|]>*<big|prod><rsub|<around|(|y,\<mu\>|)>>d*h<around*|(|U<around|(|y,\<mu\>|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The energy lies in <math|<around|[|0,12*\<ell\><rsup|4>|]>>, so this is a
  probability measure for every real <math|\<beta\>>. It is invariant under
  gauge transformations, translations and the signed permutations of the
  axes.

  <strong|Lattice schedule.>

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>Block ratios are odd integers <math|L<rsub|m>\<geqslant\>3>, with
    <math|D<rsub|m+1>=D<rsub|m>*L<rsub|m>> and <math|a<rsub|m>=1/D<rsub|m>>.

    <item>The level-<math|m> torus at volume <math|p> has
    <math|L<rsub|p>*D<rsub|m>> sites per side, with <math|4\<mid\>L<rsub|p>>.

    <item>Odd ratios make the coarse lattice compatible with the signed
    permutations.

    <item>Supports are measured by cover numbers by balls of radius <math|B>,
    which obey a coarsening inequality uniform in the volume.
  </itemize-minus>

  <strong|Group facts.> On <math|G> the unit proves:

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>a real-analytic logarithm near <math|1>;

    <item>an equivariant nearest-point retraction onto <math|G>, whose
    derivative is the orthogonal projection onto <math|\<frak-g\>>;

    <item>a curvature map <math|F<around|(|u|)>\<in\>\<frak-g\>> with
    <math|<around|\||F<around|(|u|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\<lVert\>|u-1|\<rVert\>>/<sqrt|m>>
    and <math|F*<around|(|v*u*v<rsup|-1>|)>=<math-up|Ad><around|(|v|)>*F<around|(|u|)>>.
  </itemize-minus>

  These are qualitative statements about single group elements; quantitative
  control comes in Part II.

  <strong|Role of simplicity.> Simplicity enters through one fact: every
  <math|<math-up|Ad>>-invariant symmetric bilinear form on <math|\<frak-g\>>
  is a multiple of <math|<around|\<langle\>|\<cdummy\>,\<cdummy\>|\<rangle\>><rsub|\<frak-g\>>>.
  Together with lattice symmetry, this leaves a single marginal coupling. The
  same fact is used in the one-loop computation and in the uniqueness of the
  stress tensor.

  <strong|Reflection positivity.> The Wilson measure is reflection positive
  for every <math|\<beta\>\<geqslant\>0>, for reflections through planes of
  sites and through planes between sites (the argument of Osterwalder and
  Seiler). This is proved in Part IV for the original lattice measure, not
  for an effective action, and it is used only through the chessboard
  inequality.

  <section|Part II. Fields, insertions and polymer
  activities><label|part-ii-fields-insertions-and-polymer-activities>

  <em|Units: <hlink|II.1 \V Field labels and their lattice
  representatives|outline/units/II-1.tm>; <hlink|II.2 \V Polymer activities,
  norms, the Wilson family, the construction record, reflection-positive
  cells|outline/units/II-2.tm>.>

  <strong|Labels.> In radial gauge, a connection near a point is described by
  its Taylor coefficients, and the coefficient of order <math|j> has weight
  <math|j+2>. <math|V<rsub|d>> is the space of
  <math|<math-up|Ad><around|(|G|)>>-invariant complex polynomials of weight
  at most <math|d> in these coefficients. Equivalently, it consists of the
  gauge-invariant polynomials of weight at most <math|d> in the curvature and
  its covariant derivatives. It is finite-dimensional, and
  <math|1\<in\>V<rsub|d>\<subset\>V<rsub|d+1>>.

  <strong|Lattice representatives.> The basic lattice variables are "slots":
  plaquette curvatures transported to a base point along walks.

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>For each <math|d>, finitely many slots have linearizations with no
    common kernel on the jets of weight at most <math|d>.

    <item>The slot map then has an analytic <math|<math-up|Ad>>-equivariant
    left inverse near <math|0>; it is chosen non-constructively.

    <item>Compose a basis of <math|V<rsub|d>> with the order-<math|d> Taylor
    polynomial of this inverse, and average over the signed permutations. For
    each label <math|c> this gives a gauge-invariant polynomial
    <math|\<Phi\><rsub|c>> in the link entries of a fixed box stencil.
  </itemize-minus>

  Two properties follow:

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>On links obtained by parallel transport of polynomial radial-gauge
    potentials at spacing <math|h>, <math|\<Phi\><rsub|c>> reproduces the
    classical label to order <math|h<rsup|d>>.

    <item>Suppose the plaquettes near the stencil have chord at most
    <math|t>. Then a component with <math|f\<geqslant\>1> curvature factors,
    and its derivatives, are <math|O<around|(|t<rsup|f>|)>>. Conversely, a
    function with such bounds determines a label with uniformly bounded
    coefficients.
  </itemize-minus>

  <strong|Renormalized insertions.> The insertion of <math|v\<in\>V<rsub|d>>
  at a site <math|o> is

  <\equation*>
    O<rsub|N,p><around|(|v,o|)>=a<rsub|N><rsup|-d>*<space|0.17em>\<Phi\><rsup|<around|(|d|)>><rsub|S<rsub|1/\<theta\><rsub|N>>*\<tau\><rsup|-1>*v><around|(|o|)>.
  </equation*>

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item><math|S<rsub|t>> multiplies the coefficient of a component with
    <math|f> curvature factors by <math|t<rsup|f>>.

    <item><math|\<theta\><rsub|N>> is the small-field threshold of the finest
    level.

    <item><math|\<tau\>> is a linear automorphism of <math|V<rsub|d>>. It
    commutes with conjugation and with the signed permutations, and it fixes
    the unit.
  </itemize-minus>

  The RG defines <math|\<tau\>>. The running label coordinates of an
  insertion obey a transfer relation from level <math|N> down to level
  <math|m>, and <math|\<tau\>> is their value at a reference level
  <math|r<around|(|d|)>> fixed in advance. So each insertion is normalized to
  have running coordinates exactly <math|v> at level <math|r<around|(|d|)>>.
  All labels of weight at most <math|d>, including the unit, may mix; no
  triangular structure is imposed. The large-field part of the inserted
  density goes into reserved forced polymers, so the construction never
  divides by the vacuum density.

  <strong|Equation relations.> Haar invariance gives the lattice
  Schwinger\UDyson identity <math|<around|\<langle\>|<around|\<langle\>|A,\<nabla\><rsub|e>*S<rsub|W>|\<rangle\>>*<space|0.17em>S|\<rangle\>><rsub|\<beta\>>=0>
  for <math|S> independent of the link <math|e>. Products of such forces with
  slot polynomials span the linearized free-Maxwell equation ideal, up to
  terms of higher weight. They give lattice observables whose correlations
  with distant spectators vanish exactly. Two facts are established later,
  through the comparison with free theory: these relations become null in the
  limit, and they exhaust <math|N<rsub|d>>.

  <strong|Polymers, budgets and marks.>

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>A polymer activity has a finite support of sites.

    <\itemize-minus>
      <item>Regular activities are anchored at a site and measured by suprema
      of derivatives over small-field configurations.

      <item>Forced activities carry the large-field regions, together with an
      inventory of flagged sites.
    </itemize-minus>

    <item>Activities are controlled by budgets summed through each site,
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|X\<ni\>x>e<rsup|a<space|0.17em><math-up|cov><rsub|B><around|(|X|)>><around|\<lVert\>|K<rsub|X>|\<rVert\>>\<leqslant\><text|budget>>.

    <item>Insertions carry marks: commuting variables <math|\<lambda\>> with
    <math|\<lambda\><rsup|2>=0>, one for each label or site that may occur.
    Exponentials of marked quantities are finite sums, and logarithms are
    finite recursions in the number of marks.

    <item>The weighted norm <math|<around|\<lVert\>|a|\<rVert\>><rsub|w>=<big|sum><rsub|x><big|prod><rsub|\<lambda\>\<in\>x>w<rsub|\<lambda\>><space|0.17em><around|\||a<around|(|x|)>|\|>>
    is submultiplicative, so bounds do not grow with the number of marks.

    <item>A tree-graph bound without factorials controls connected clusters
    of overlapping supports.
  </itemize-minus>

  <section|Part III. The ultraviolet construction><label|part-iii-the-ultraviolet-construction>

  <subsection|The block-spin step and the background
  field><label|the-block-spin-step-and-the-background-field>

  <em|Units: <hlink|III.1 \V The block-spin step and the symmetric gauge
  fixing|outline/units/III-1.tm>; <hlink|III.6 \V The stationary branch:
  background field of the effective action|outline/units/III-6.tm>;
  <hlink|III.3 \V Free lattice kernels and
  covariances|outline/units/III-3.tm>.>

  At level <math|m>, relative to product Haar measure, the density is

  <\equation*>
    \<rho\><rsub|m><around|(|U|)>=exp <around*|(|v\|\<Lambda\><rsub|m>\|-\<beta\>*<space|0.17em>\<cal-K\><around|(|U|)>-\<cal-L\><around|(|U|)>-\<cal-E\><around|(|U|)>|)>*<space|0.17em>\<cal-G\><around|(|U|)>.
  </equation*>

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item><math|\<cal-K\>> is built from lattice paths: a plaquette term,
    smeared curvature squares, and cubic and quartic polynomials in
    transported curvatures. It is averaged over the signed permutations and
    normalized so that its quadratic symbol at zero momentum is the identity;
    this fixes the meaning of <math|\<beta\>>.

    <item><math|\<cal-L\>> is a loop term independent of <math|\<beta\>>.

    <item><math|\<cal-E\>> is the sum of the regular activities times
    small-field indicators.

    <item><math|\<cal-G\>> is the gas of forced activities.

    <item>At the finest level, <math|\<rho\><rsub|N>=e<rsup|-\<beta\><rsub|N,p,N>*S<rsub|W>>>.
  </itemize-minus>

  <strong|The kernel.> One step is <math|\<rho\><rsub|m><around|(|V|)>=<big|int>k<rsub|\<beta\>><around|(|V,U|)>*<space|0.17em>\<rho\><rsub|m+1><around|(|U|)>*<space|0.17em>d*U>.
  Given the fine configuration <math|U>, a finite tag <math|t> is drawn with
  probability <math|\<lambda\><around|(|U,t|)>>. Each coarse link is then
  drawn independently, with Haar density

  <\equation*>
    Z<rsub|\<beta\>><rsup|-1>*exp <around*|(|-\<beta\>*\<kappa\>*<around*|(|1-<tfrac|1|m><math-up|Re><space|0.17em><math-up|tr><space|0.17em>V<rsub|b>*Q<rsub|b><around|(|U,t|)><rsup|-1>|)>|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<kappa\>\<gtr\>0> is the penalty. The predictor
  <math|Q<rsub|b>> is built in two steps:

  <\enumerate>
    <item>On a patch of radius <math|<around|(|J+3|)>*L> around each coarse
    site, a gauge frame is chosen.

    <\itemize-minus>
      <item>If all route holonomies on the patch are close to <math|1>, the
      frame is the unique minimizer of an averaged holonomy discrepancy.

      <item>Otherwise it is a uniformly drawn eligible axial gauge; such
      gauges exist for every configuration.
    </itemize-minus>

    <item>Fine transports of length <math|L>, read in these frames, are
    combined by a nonlinear (minimizing) average with a <math|J>-fold
    convolution weight, with a random fallback.
  </enumerate>

  Properties of the kernel:

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>Haar invariance gives <math|<big|int>k<rsub|\<beta\>><around|(|V,U|)>*<space|0.17em>d*V=1>
    exactly.

    <item>The kernel is exactly covariant under fine gauge transformations
    and the lattice symmetries.

    <item>Hence <math|<big|int>\<rho\><rsub|m>> equals the Wilson partition
    function at every level, and the step is defined without any small-field
    assumption.
  </itemize-minus>

  <strong|Background field.> For the integration, the links of a spanning
  forest are set to <math|1>, and each remaining link is <math|exp
  <around|(|a|)>> times a fixed lift of the coarse field <math|x>. The
  background is the critical branch <math|a=\<rho\><around|(|x|)>> of the
  classical Taylor model of the action (quadratic, cubic and quartic terms)
  plus the blocking penalty, at fixed <math|x>. The steps are:

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>The Hessian at the flat field is a positive "bed" form plus the
    penalty.

    <item>Each non-tree link is bounded by the curvature in its block plus
    the response of the predictor. With this, the Hessian is uniformly
    positive in the vertical directions, with a constant independent of the
    volume and the forest.

    <item>The analytic implicit function theorem then gives <math|\<rho\>> as
    a locally unique analytic germ with explicit linear part. Statements with
    a uniform radius and localized jets give control independent of the
    volume.

    <item>The branch is not shown to be a global minimizer, and this is not
    used.

    <item>Changing the forest multiplies the Hessian determinant by the
    square of a Jacobian and the Haar measure by the Jacobian itself. So the
    critical value and the one-loop term do not depend on the forest.
  </itemize-minus>

  <strong|Free kernels.> The free covariances are written in Fourier space
  through the actual averaging filters, which are entire and multiplicative
  under composition of blockings. Aliased momenta carry extra powers of
  <math|1/L>.

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>All constants (decay rates, strip widths, lower bounds) are fixed
    before the block ratio <math|L>.

    <item>The quadratic forms decay exponentially and are bounded below
    uniformly in the volume.

    <item>An exact identity on each pair of tori splits a quadratic form into
    a coarse part and a fluctuation part (a Schur complement).
  </itemize-minus>

  <subsection|Gaussian integration and the cluster
  expansion><label|gaussian-integration-and-the-cluster-expansion>

  <em|Units: <hlink|III.4 \V Gaussian integration: change of Gaussian sector,
  determinants, window law|outline/units/III-4.tm>; <hlink|III.5 \V Cluster
  expansion of the polymer gas|outline/units/III-5.tm>; <hlink|III.8 \V
  Connected activities and backward dependence
  cones|outline/units/III-8.tm>.>

  <strong|Change of variables.> Active links are written in exponential
  coordinates around the background. Inactive links stay in Haar measure.

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>The change of variables to a Gaussian integral with covariance
    <math|C> and mean <math|m<around|(|Y|)>> is an exact identity. It holds
    for any positive-definite <math|C>, keeps all Jacobians, and uses no
    smallness assumption.

    <item>The covariance actually used is the inverse of the gauge-fixed Gram
    form on the active links, not the inverse of the Wilson quadratic form.
    Its precision is bounded below uniformly in the volume.

    <item>Window law: the global inverse is approximated by inverses on
    windows of radius <math|r>, with error <math|C*e<rsup|-v*r>> uniform in
    the volume.

    <item><math|log det> separates into local terms and far-field terms with
    summable errors.
  </itemize-minus>

  <strong|Cluster expansion.> The gas is <math|\<Xi\><around|(|w|)>=<big|sum><rsub|s><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>s>w<rsub|i>>,
  summed over families of polymers with pairwise disjoint supports and real
  weights. With <math|B=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
  <wide|\<beta\>|^>|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>>, the smallness condition is

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|i:<space|0.17em>x\<in\>X<rsub|i>><around|\||w<rsub|i>|\|>*<space|0.17em>e<rsup|<around|(|a+\<delta\>|)><space|0.17em><math-up|cov><rsub|B><around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>>\<leqslant\>\<gamma\><text|for
    every site >x,<space|2em>4*<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>*\<gamma\>/\<delta\>\<leqslant\>q\<less\>1.
  </equation*>

  Under this condition:

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item><math|log \<Xi\>> is an absolutely convergent sum of connected
    clusters, bounded through spanning trees (Penrose's partition scheme);

    <item>removing every polymer that meets a set <math|Y> changes <math|log
    \<Xi\>> by at most <math|<around|\||Y|\|>*\<gamma\>/<around|(|1-q|)>>,
    uniformly in the volume.
  </itemize-minus>

  So the vacuum logarithm is extensive, but a local change costs only its
  size. A second, graph-free argument based on a deletion recursion gives
  bounds of the same type.

  <strong|Connected activities and dependence cones.>

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>Fixed-order connected coefficients come from the heat equation
    <math|\<partial\><rsub|t>*W=<tfrac|1|2><around|(|\<Gamma\>:\<nabla\><rsup|2>*W+\<Gamma\>:\<nabla\>*W*<space|0.17em>\<nabla\>*W|)>>
    for <math|W<rsub|t>=log \<bbb-E\><rsub|t*\<Gamma\>>*e<rsup|V>>. A rooted
    <math|\<ell\><rsup|1>> norm with weight
    <math|e<rsup|\<sigma\><space|0.17em><math-up|diam>>> makes contraction
    and joining lossless, so these coefficients are polynomially bounded
    uniformly in the volume. There is no resummation.

    <item>Pulling a coarse observable back through the block kernel gives a
    function of the fine links in a window of fixed radius around its
    support. The backward cone therefore grows by a bounded physical width
    per step.

    <item>For disjoint supports, the pull-back factorizes exactly.
  </itemize-minus>

  <subsection|Large fields and one nonlinear
  step><label|large-fields-and-one-nonlinear-step>

  <em|Unit: <hlink|III.10 \V One full nonlinear renormalization step: bounds
  on irrelevant terms|outline/units/III-10.tm>.>

  At reference coupling <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^>> the small-field threshold is

  <\equation*>
    t=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsup|-1/2>*P<around|(|<wide|\<beta\>|^>|)>,<space|2em>P<around|(|<wide|\<beta\>|^>|)>=exp
    <around*|(|<around|(|log log <wide|\<beta\>|^>|)><rsup|2>|)>,
  </equation*>

  well above the Gaussian fluctuation scale
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsup|-1/2>>.

  <strong|Seeds.> A seed is one of:

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>a fine plaquette with chord at least <math|t/4>;

    <item>a coarse link on which the coarse field departs from the predictor
    by more than a fixed multiple of <math|t>;

    <item>a flagged coarse site.
  </itemize-minus>

  <strong|Energy lower bound.> For every store <math|A> of seeds,

  <\equation*>
    \<kappa\>*<space|0.17em>t<rsup|2>*<space|0.17em>\<sharp\>*A\<leqslant\><text|quadratic
    energy of the step integrand, after subtracting the stationary
    predictor>.
  </equation*>

  The energy is a sum over five channels, one of which is the blocking
  penalty. The proof assigns charges to fine cells with bounded multiplicity,
  and controls the linearization defects on cells without seeds.

  <strong|Why this suffices.> Since <math|\<beta\>*t<rsup|2>\<simeq\>P<rsup|2>>,
  each seed gains a factor <math|e<rsup|-c*P<rsup|2>>>. <math|P<rsup|2>>
  grows faster than any power of <math|log <wide|\<beta\>|^>>, so this gain
  beats:

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>the polylogarithmic number of connected seed sets through a site;

    <item>any per-seed loss of a fixed power of <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^>>, or
    of order <math|P<rsup|z>> with <math|z\<less\>2>.
  </itemize-minus>

  The decomposition into large-field sectors is exact: large-field sectors
  are integrated, not discarded.

  <strong|One step.> The step takes inputs at level <math|m+1>: regular norms
  at most <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsup|-11/10>>, forced norms at most
  <math|e<rsup|-P<rsup|1/5>>>, and the coupling in the band
  <math|<around|[|<wide|\<beta\>|^>/2,2*<wide|\<beta\>|^>|]>>. For <math|L>
  large, and then <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^>> large, it produces:

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>site-summable budgets for the new regular activities;

    <item>the bound <math|exp <around*|(|<around|(|C<space|0.17em><math-up|bill><rsup|5>-\<beta\>*\<mu\>*t<rsup|2>|)>*\<sharp\>*A|)>>
    for each large-field sector, where <math|<math-up|bill>=1+B+<around|(|log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^>|)><rsup|2>>;

    <item>bounds on the probabilities of the remaining catastrophic events.
  </itemize-minus>

  The marginal and vacuum terms are extracted, and the level-<math|m> bounds
  restored, by the reset below.

  <subsection|The marginal coupling and the
  schedule><label|the-marginal-coupling-and-the-schedule>

  <em|Units: <hlink|III.7 \V Matching the Wilson energy to fourth
  order|outline/units/III-7.tm>; <hlink|III.9 \V The marginal flow: the
  running coupling and its reset|outline/units/III-9.tm>; <hlink|III.2 \V
  Choosing the constants: the order of choice and the RG
  schedule|outline/units/III-2.tm>.>

  <strong|Matching and the one-loop coefficient.>

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>At the finest level, a path representation is constructed whose
    jets of order at most 4 agree with those of the Wilson energy in
    exponential coordinates.

    <item>At later levels, the cubic and quartic parts are represented in
    curvature variables by quasi-local lifts (a cost-controlled Poincaré
    lemma).

    <item>In each step, the coefficient <math|\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>> of
    curvature squared produced by the one-loop term satisfies
    <math|<around|\||\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>+\<kappa\><rsub|G>*log
    L<rsub|m>|\|>\<leqslant\>D>, with <math|D> fixed before <math|L>.
  </itemize-minus>

  The value of <math|\<kappa\><rsub|G>> comes from an explicit momentum
  integral. The comparison symbol is

  <\equation*>
    h<around|(|k|)>=4*C<rsub|\<frak-g\>>*<around*|(|<frac|k<rsub|1><rsup|2>|<around|\||k|\|><rsup|6>>-<frac|4*k<rsub|1><rsup|2>*k<rsub|0><rsup|2>|<around|\||k|\|><rsup|8>>-<frac|1|<around|\||k|\|><rsup|4>>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Its angular average is <math|4*C<rsub|\<frak-g\>>*<around|(|<tfrac|1|4>-<tfrac|1|6>-1|)><around|\||k|\|><rsup|-4>=-<tfrac|11|3>C<rsub|\<frak-g\>><around|\||k|\|><rsup|-4>>,
  and <math|<big|int><rsub|1/L\<less\><around|\||k|\|>\<leqslant\>1><around|\||k|\|><rsup|-4>*<space|0.17em>d<rsup|4>*k=2*\<pi\><rsup|2>*log
  L>. Hence <math|<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|-4>*<big|int><rsub|1/L\<less\><around|\||k|\|>\<leqslant\>1>h=-\<kappa\><rsub|G>*log
  L>. Comparing the step's Hessian with <math|h> up to
  <math|O<around|(|<around|\||k|\|><rsup|-3>|)>> gives the bound on
  <math|\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>>.

  <strong|The reset.> After the step, the blocked density is rewritten
  exactly, at every configuration including large fields:

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>a new coupling <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,m>=\<beta\><rsub|N,p,m+1>+\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>+\<delta\>>;

    <item>a new vacuum constant;

    <item>reorganized activities: flat values and isotropic quadratic parts
    are subtracted, compensating path terms are added, and mismatches of the
    small-field masks go into the forced gas.
  </itemize-minus>

  The correction <math|\<delta\>> is the isotropic Hessian coefficient of the
  regular activities:

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>By simplicity and hyperoctahedral symmetry, their quadratic part at
    a point is <math|\<delta\>> times the identity on plaquette orientations.

    <item><math|<around|\||\<delta\>|\|>\<leqslant\>1>, and <math|\<delta\>>
    is fixed by a scalar equation with a unique root.
  </itemize-minus>

  Altogether, with <math|\<omega\><rsub|j>> a drift allowance attached to
  regularity index <math|j>,

  <\equation*>
    <mid|\|>\<beta\><rsub|N,p,m+1>-\<beta\><rsub|N,p,m>-\<kappa\><rsub|G>*log
    L<rsub|m><mid|\|>\<leqslant\>\<omega\><rsub|J<rsub|m+1>>.
  </equation*>

  <strong|Endpoint.> For real <math|<around|\||b|\|>\<less\>r<rsub|\<ast\>>>
  there is a trajectory with level-<math|0> coupling
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>+b>. Its bare coupling is unique within the
  construction, and the trajectory continues analytically to complex
  <math|<around|\||b|\|>\<less\>r<rsub|\<ast\>>>, with <math|r<rsub|\<ast\>>>
  independent of <math|N> and <math|p>. The family uses <math|b=0>, so
  <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,0>=<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>> for all <math|N> and
  <math|p>. An intermediate-value shooting argument also produces such
  trajectories; the analytic version is the one used for convergence at fixed
  volume below.

  <strong|Running.> Put <math|s<rsub|m>=log <around|(|1/a<rsub|m>|)>> and
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>=<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>+\<kappa\><rsub|G>*s<rsub|m>>.
  Induction from level <math|0> gives <math|<around|\||\<beta\><rsub|N,p,m>-<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>|\|>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|k\<leqslant\>m>\<omega\><rsub|J<rsub|k>>>,
  and the schedule makes this <math|o<around|(|s<rsub|m>|)>>:

  <\equation*>
    \<beta\><rsub|N,p,m>=<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>+\<kappa\><rsub|G>*log
    <around|(|1/a<rsub|m>|)>+o<around*|(|log
    <around|(|1/a<rsub|m>|)>|)><space|2em><text|uniformly in
    >N\<geqslant\>m<text| and >p.
  </equation*>

  At <math|m=N> this is the slope law with
  <math|b<rsub|\<ast\>>=\<kappa\><rsub|G>>. Since
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>> is chosen large, every level stays in the
  band <math|<around|[|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>/2,2*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>|]>>.
  The statement is leading-logarithmic only: there is no two-loop term.

  <strong|Why the block ratios grow.> The one-loop error per step is bounded
  but does not decrease with <math|L>. For the accumulated error to be
  <math|o<around|(|s<rsub|m>|)>>, the allowance per unit of logarithmic scale
  must tend to zero. The schedule therefore imposes two conditions:

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item><math|\<omega\><rsub|J<rsub|m+1>>\<leqslant\>J<rsub|m+1><rsup|-2>*log
    L<rsub|m>>, which forces <math|log L<rsub|m>\<to\>\<infty\>>;

    <item><math|log L<rsub|m>/s<rsub|m>\<to\>0>, which keeps each step small
    compared with the total scale.
  </itemize-minus>

  The ratios are constant while the regularity index is fixed,
  <math|L<rsub|m>=\<ell\><rsub|J<rsub|m+1>>>. The regularity index <math|J>
  is nondecreasing, rises by at most one per level, tends to infinity toward
  the fine end, and stays constant on long windows of levels. One sequence
  <math|<around|(|L<rsub|m>|)>> serves every cutoff <math|N>.

  <strong|Order of choices.> The constants are chosen in a fixed order:

  <\enumerate>
    <item>the kernel with its penalty, and for every index <math|j> its
    regularity profile (derivative order, spatial power, support exponent,
    widths, path counts, drift allowance), together with the one-loop error
    constant <math|D>;

    <item>all block ratios <math|\<ell\><rsub|j>>, so that the allowances and
    the logarithms of the combinatorial costs are at most
    <math|j<rsup|-2>*log \<ell\><rsub|j>>;

    <item>the smallness exponents;

    <item>the entry couplings and the volume conditions;

    <item><math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>>;

    <item>the assignment of regularity profiles to levels.
  </enumerate>

  Each degree <math|d> has a reference level <math|r<around|(|d|)>> with
  regularity index at least <math|d+3>. Beyond <math|r<around|(|d|)>> the
  derivative order exceeds <math|d> and the spatial power is at least
  <math|d>. All choices precede any observable or extraction order, so every
  degree is served by the same family.

  <subsection|Renormalized observables, uniformly in the
  volume><label|renormalized-observables-uniformly-in-the-volume>

  <em|Units: <hlink|III.11 \V Comparison with the free Gaussian measure:
  interacting rank and determinant budgets|outline/units/III-11.tm>;
  <hlink|III.12 \V Bounds uniform in the volume and convergence as the
  spacing tends to zero|outline/units/III-12.tm>; <hlink|III.15 \V Cost
  accounting for observables and the remainder
  comparison|outline/units/III-15.tm>; <hlink|III.16 \V Analytic
  toolkit|outline/units/III-16.tm>.>

  <strong|The chessboard root.> The quantity propagated for observables is
  the chessboard root. For a function <math|F> of the links in one cell of a
  tiling of the torus by cells of side <math|a<rsub|m>>,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<lVert\>|F|\<rVert\>><rsub|T>=<mid|\|><space|0.17em>\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<beta\><rsub|N,p>><around*|[|<big|prod><rsub|cells><text|reflected
    copies of >F|]><mid|\|><rsup|1/\<sharp\><text|cells>>.
  </equation*>

  The main bound reads as follows. For every degree <math|d>, radius <math|R>
  and <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0> there is <math|A> such that, for every
  <math|m\<geqslant\>r<around|(|d|)>>, there is <math|N<rsub|0>> independent
  of <math|p> with

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<lVert\>|O<rsub|N,p><around|(|v,o|)>|\<rVert\>><rsub|T<rsub|m><around|(|o|)>>\<leqslant\>A*<space|0.17em>a<rsub|m><rsup|-d-\<varepsilon\>><space|2em><around|(|N\<geqslant\>N<rsub|0>,<text|all
    >p,<around|\<lVert\>|v|\<rVert\>>\<leqslant\>R|)>.
  </equation*>

  It is proved by running the insertion through the same RG steps;
  <math|\<varepsilon\>> absorbs losses smaller than any power. Reflection
  positivity enters only afterwards, through the chessboard inequality. That
  inequality makes the root a seminorm and bounds correlations of separated
  observables by products of roots.

  <strong|No loss of <math|log \<beta\>> per site.> A root over <math|c>
  copies tolerates a fixed cost per copy, but not a factor <math|log
  \<beta\>> per site. The determinant budget avoids this by a gauge count on
  a terminal torus with <math|c=L<rsup|4>> sites:

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>A tree gauge leaves <math|4*c-<around|(|c-1|)>=3*c+1> free links.

    <item>In the numerator, <math|3*c-3> of them are integrated one at a
    time, each being the only new link of a chosen plaquette. Each integral
    gives at most <math|b*<space|0.17em>E<rsup|dim G>>, with
    <math|E\<propto\>g=\<beta\><rsup|-1/2>>. The four links that wrap around
    the torus, one per axis, are left over.

    <item>The normalization is bounded below by confining all <math|3*c+1>
    free links to Haar balls of radius <math|g/4>.

    <item>The ratio is therefore at most <math|exp
    <around|(|c*<space|0.17em>\<pi\>-4*dim G*<space|0.17em>log g|)>>, where
    the cost <math|\<pi\>> per site is independent of <math|g>.

    <item>Since the number of copies is <math|c>, the root carries only
    <math|g<rsup|-4*dim G/c>>.
  </itemize-minus>

  <strong|Field content.> Free Gaussian detectors give uniform rank
  detection: <math|<around|\<lVert\>|\<pi\>*x|\<rVert\>>\<leqslant\>Z<space|0.17em><around|\<lVert\>|<math-up|probe><around|(|\<pi\>*x|)>|\<rVert\>>>,
  where <math|\<pi\>> is the projection onto quotient labels. The limit field
  spaces are isomorphic, degree by degree, to those of free Maxwell theory,
  compatibly with conjugation and reflections.

  <strong|Difference quotients.>

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>A normalized lattice difference of an insertion of degree <math|d>
    equals an insertion <math|O<around|(|x|)>> of degree
    <math|d+<around|\||l|\|>>, up to a term whose root tends to zero.

    <item>Write <math|x=\<pi\>*x+<around|(|x-\<pi\>*x|)>>. The part
    <math|x-\<pi\>*x> lies in the Maxwell equation ideal and has small root
    by the lattice equations of motion. The part <math|\<pi\>*x> is bounded
    by the rank detector.

    <item>So difference quotients satisfy root bounds of the same form. By
    the chessboard inequality, lattice correlations with any number of
    differences are bounded at <math|r>-separated points, uniformly in
    <math|p>.
  </itemize-minus>

  These bounds are propagated through the RG; they do not come from
  differentiating a sup norm.

  <strong|A rank-free trace bound.> Let <math|X\<geqslant\>0> be a matrix and
  let the insertions <math|b<rsub|a>> be separated by gaps of more than
  <math|2*r> factors of <math|X>. Moving <math|m\<leqslant\>r> factors from
  one gap to another changes <math|<math-up|tr><big|prod><rsub|a>b<rsub|a>*X<rsup|Y<rsub|a>>>
  by at most <math|<around|(|<math-up|cost>/r|)>*<space|0.17em>m>. The cost
  is a product of trace norms with no dimension factor. The proof is a Hölder
  inequality for complex exponents, obtained from Hadamard's three-lines
  theorem, followed by a Cauchy estimate. By quadrature it extends to
  continuous positive kernels. Part IV uses it for equicontinuity.

  <strong|Bookkeeping.> The numerical conditions of all steps can be met in
  one fixed order: constants before <math|L>, then <math|L>, then the later
  constants, then <math|\<beta\>> large. This holds uniformly across
  regularity profiles.

  <subsection|Convergence at fixed volume and the construction
  run><label|convergence-at-fixed-volume-and-the-construction-run>

  <em|Units: <hlink|III.12|outline/units/III-12.tm>; <hlink|III.13 \V
  Extension to all labels: one common Wilson family|outline/units/III-13.tm>;
  <hlink|III.14 \V The construction run: flat representations and the
  ultraviolet rate|outline/units/III-14.tm>.>

  <strong|The obstacle.> At fixed volume, two cutoffs <math|N> and
  <math|N<rprime|'>> have different bare couplings, each fixed by the
  condition <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,0>=<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>>. Continuity
  in a fixed bare coupling therefore compares nothing.

  <strong|Fixed-level convergence and tails.> The construction has a
  one-parameter family of trajectories:

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>The level-<math|0> coupling is <math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>+w*s>,
    with a real tuning parameter <math|s\<in\><around|[|-1,1|]>>. For each
    <math|s> the bare coupling is unique within the recursion.

    <item>The bare coupling and effective data have analytic dependence on
    the tuning parameter. The estimates on that dependence are separate from
    the Cauchy argument below.

    <item>For fixed <math|j>, convergence of the trajectory and stability of
    the observable transfers give convergence of the effective insertions and
    their polymer parts. Uniform convergence passes through the Haar
    integrals, and a positive lower bound on the normalized partition
    function gives convergent correlation approximations.

    <item>The omitted part is bounded, uniformly in the cutoff, by
    <math|2*F<rsub|0>*e<rsup|-B<rsub|j>/64>>, where
    <math|B<rsub|j>=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|j>|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>\<to\>\<infty\>>.

    <item>Choose <math|j> to make this tail small, then take the cutoffs
    large enough that their level-<math|j> approximations are close. This
    proves the Cauchy property first at lattice-fitted configurations;
    equicontinuity extends it to all configurations distinct modulo the
    period. See <hlink|III.12, ŸŸ4.5\U4.6|outline/notes/III-12.tm#45-convergence-in-the-mesh>.
  </itemize-minus>

  Combined with the difference bounds, this gives at each fixed volume:

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>limit kernels smooth away from periodic coincidences;

    <item>convergence of difference quotients to derivatives;

    <item>convergence of smeared moments along the whole sequence of
    spacings.
  </itemize-minus>

  <strong|The family used by the public theorem.>
  <hlink|VIII.1|outline/units/VIII-1.tm> assembles the construction for one
  family, using the bounds and convergence results described in
  III.9\UIII.12. <hlink|III.13|outline/units/III-13.tm> describes an
  alternative coordinated family with root bounds, an analytic shooting
  family and Maxwell rank comparisons. Its existence statements do not
  identify that family with the one used by <verbatim|YangMills.constructed>;
  properties from the two constructions should not be combined without such
  an identification.

  <strong|The construction run (III.14).> III.14 packages the construction
  run. It also proves quantitative convergence of the order-zero quadratic
  part of the effective action (its position-space Hessian kernel) at each
  fixed level <math|i>, at rate <math|<around|(|a<rsub|N>/a<rsub|i>|)><rsup|c>>.

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>The difference between two cutoffs is written in curvature
    variables as <math|d<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*A*d>, with <math|A> defined modulo
    kernels annihilated by this map.

    <item>So the curvature precision, which has zero modes, is never
    inverted.

    <item>This rate concerns the quadratic germ only, not the full effective
    action or the final limits.
  </itemize-minus>

  <section|Part IV. Infinite volume><label|part-iv-infinite-volume>

  <em|Unit: <hlink|IV.1 \V Infinite volume: extraction of a subsequence and
  the Euclidean axioms of the limit|outline/units/IV-1.tm>.>

  <strong|Local bounds.>

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>Reflection positivity gives the chessboard inequality
    <math|<around|\||<around|\<langle\>|<big|prod><rsub|c>F<rsub|c>|\<rangle\>>|\|>\<leqslant\><big|prod><rsub|c><around|\<lVert\>|F<rsub|c>|\<rVert\>><rsub|T>>
    for every <math|\<beta\>\<geqslant\>0>. Log-convexity of roots under
    translation handles cells not centred at the insertion.

    <item>Put each insertion in its own cell of side
    <math|a<rsub|m>\<less\>r>. Then <math|<around|\||C<rsub|N,p><around|(|v;x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>A<rsup|n>>
    whenever each pair of points has some coordinate at distance at least
    <math|r>, uniformly in <math|p> and for large <math|N>.

    <item>Near collisions, the same argument gives
    <math|<around|\||S<around|(|v;x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*r<rsup|-4*n*<around|(|1+max
    d<rsub|i>|)>>> when all points are at mutual distance at least <math|r>.
  </itemize-minus>

  <strong|Equicontinuity.> Let <math|<math-up|Gen><rsub|n>> be the set of
  configurations whose points have pairwise distinct coordinates on every
  axis.

  <\enumerate>
    <item>Integrating out the links along one axis gives a positive transfer
    kernel <math|K>. A correlation becomes
    <math|<math-up|Tr><big|prod><rsub|k>B<rsub|k>*K<rsup|Y<rsub|k>>/<math-up|Tr><space|0.17em>K<rsup|M>>,
    with slab kernels <math|B<rsub|k>> and gaps <math|Y<rsub|k>>.

    <item>Moving one point shifts units between two gaps. The trace is entire
    in the size of the shift.

    <item>The rank-free Hölder bound controls the trace on a disc, and a
    Cauchy estimate gives a Lipschitz bound.

    <item>The trace norms of the slab kernels are themselves chessboard roots
    of <math|2*q> alternating copies, so the bound is uniform in the volume.
  </enumerate>

  The result is <math|<around|\||C<around|(|x|)>-C<around|(|y|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>16*n*A<rsup|n>*<around|\||x-y|\|>/R>
  on compact subsets of <math|<math-up|Gen><rsub|n>> whose coordinate gaps
  exceed <math|R>. No derivative bound is used.

  <strong|Extraction.> Arzelà\UAscoli is applied twice, over countably many
  degrees, basis labels and compact sets: first to extract a spacing
  subsequence that works for all volumes, then a volume subsequence. This
  gives limit kernels on <math|<math-up|Gen><rsub|n>>. The complement
  <math|<math-up|Conf><rsub|n>\<setminus\><math-up|Gen><rsub|n>> is a null
  set, and the kernels are locally bounded on <math|<math-up|Conf><rsub|n>>,
  so dominated convergence gives the smeared limits.

  <strong|Derivatives and smoothness.>

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>Exact summation by parts in the lattice positions moves a
    difference quotient onto the test function.

    <item>The ultraviolet estimates show that the lattice difference quotient
    of an insertion is chessboard-close to an insertion of one degree higher.

    <item>A dimension count, from the rank comparison, identifies the kernel
    of the limiting projections with the null labels.

    <item>Finite-dimensional compactness then gives fixed derivative maps
    <math|\<partial\><rsub|a>:F<rsub|d>\<to\>F<rsub|d+1>> with
  </itemize-minus>

  <\equation*>
    \<partial\><rsub|x<rsub|i><rsup|a>>*S<around|(|\<ldots\>,v<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>;x|)>=S<around|(|\<ldots\>,\<partial\><rsub|a>*v<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>;x|)><space|2em><text|in
    >\<cal-D\><rprime|'><around|(|<math-up|Conf><rsub|n>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Iteration and a Fourier argument give <math|S\<in\>C<rsup|\<infty\>><around|(|<math-up|Conf><rsub|n>|)>>,
  with derivatives polynomially bounded near collisions.

  <strong|What the limit inherits.> The limit inherits:

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>translations;

    <item>the group of signed permutations of the axes, acting on points and
    labels;

    <item>permutation symmetry, reality and the unit label;

    <item>reflection positivity at coordinate planes, for point and smeared
    tests.
  </itemize-minus>

  It does not inherit continuous rotations, clustering or uniqueness.

  <strong|Removing the spacing refinement.> The extraction refines both the
  spacing and the volume sequences. At each admissible fixed volume, the
  smeared limit along the whole spacing sequence exists (Part III) and agrees
  with the subsequential one. This removes the spacing refinement from the
  final statement.

  <section|Part V. Phases and relativistic
  reconstruction><label|part-v-phases-and-relativistic-reconstruction>

  <em|Units: <hlink|V.1 \V Decomposition of the limit into
  phases|outline/units/V-1.tm>; <hlink|V.2 \V Osterwalder\USchrader
  reconstruction of a Wightman theory|outline/units/V-2.tm>; <hlink|V.3 \V
  Reconstruction for every phase and the reconstructed
  theory|outline/units/V-3.tm>.>

  <strong|The OS space of the mixture.> Positive-time field words, modulo the
  null space of <math|<around|\<langle\>|\<theta\>*w,w<rprime|'>|\<rangle\>><rsub|S>>
  and completed, form a Hilbert space. It carries:

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>a contraction semigroup <math|e<rsup|-t*H>> with
    <math|H\<geqslant\>0>;

    <item>a unitary representation of the spatial translations.
  </itemize-minus>

  The joint spectrum lies in a half-space. No positive spectral gap is
  asserted. The zero-energy space may be large. Its projection <math|\<Pi\>>
  is the strong limit of <math|e<rsup|-t*H>> as <math|t\<to\>\<infty\>>.

  <strong|Thin slabs.> A word <math|w> placed in a thin time slab defines an
  operator <math|B> on the OS space. To bound it, consider the train
  <math|v<rsub|n+1>=B<rsup|\<ast\>>*B*v<rsub|n>>, which contains <math|n>
  reflected copies of <math|w> and an arbitrary exterior word.

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>The lattice bounds give <math|<around|\<lVert\>|v<rsub|n>|\<rVert\>><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>B<rsub|v>*A<rsup|4*n>>.
    Here <math|A> is polynomial in the word data and in the inverse buffer
    width, and <math|B<rsub|v>> depends on the exterior word but not on
    <math|n>.

    <item>A power lemma removes <math|B<rsub|v>>: if
    <math|<around|\<lVert\>|<around|(|B*A|)><rsup|n>*v|\<rVert\>>\<leqslant\>K<rsub|v>*D<rsup|2*n>>
    for all <math|n>, then <math|<around|\<lVert\>|A*v|\<rVert\>>\<leqslant\>D<around|\<lVert\>|v|\<rVert\>>>.
  </itemize-minus>

  So the exterior word is paid once and disappears under the <math|n>-th
  root.

  <strong|Phases.>

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>The compressions <math|M<around|(|w|)>=\<Pi\>*<space|0.17em>B<rsub|w>\|<rsub|ker
    H>> are independent of the width and placement of the slab.

    <item><math|<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,M<around|(|w<rsub|1>|)>*\<cdots\>*M<around|(|w<rsub|k>|)>*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>>
    is the limit of <math|S> as the clusters are moved apart in time order.
  </itemize-minus>

  The compressions commute:

  <\enumerate>
    <item>Place four clusters on the rays
    <math|n*<around|(|u*e<rsub|4>+v*e<rsub|1>|)>>, with
    <math|<around|(|u,v|)>=<around|(|0,0|)>,<around|(|1,2|)>,<around|(|2,1|)>,<around|(|3,3|)>>.

    <item>Exchanging the time axis with a spatial axis reverses the order of
    the middle pair.

    <item><math|O<around|(|4|)>> covariance and permutation symmetry of
    <math|S> then give <math|<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,M<rsub|x>*M<rsub|y>*M<rsub|w>*M<rsub|z>*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>=<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,M<rsub|x>*M<rsub|w>*M<rsub|y>*M<rsub|z>*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>>.

    <item>By density, <math|<around|[|M<rsub|y>,M<rsub|w>|]>=0>.
  </enumerate>

  The resulting phase decomposition:

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>The <math|M<around|(|w|)>> generate a commutative unital
    <math|C<rsup|\<ast\>>>-algebra. Its Gelfand spectrum is <math|X>, which
    is compact and metrizable.

    <item><math|\<nu\>> represents the vacuum state, and it has full support
    because the vacuum is separating.

    <item>The phase correlations are <math|S<rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|w|)>=\<xi\><around|(|M<around|(|w|)>|)>>.
    They are continuous in <math|\<xi\>>, and
    <math|<big|int>S<rsup|\<xi\>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\>=S<rsup|<math-up|mix>>>.

    <item>Reflection positivity, <math|O<around|(|4|)>> invariance, the
    derivative and stress identities and uniform growth bounds hold for every
    <math|\<xi\>>. They are proved as operator identities before any
    character is applied.

    <item>Every phase has the same null spaces as the mixture.

    <item>A variance argument over a countable total family of words gives a
    one-dimensional zero-energy space for <math|\<nu\>>-almost every phase.

    <item>The selected phase <math|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>> is one such phase,
    chosen by this existence argument; no further condition is imposed. The
    trace law of Part VII is proved for the phase so chosen.
  </itemize-minus>

  <strong|Reconstruction.> For each phase, slab operators bounded by
  <math|C*<space|0.17em>p<rsup|-A>> (with <math|p> the slab half-width)
  define

  <\equation*>
    F<rsub|n><around|(|z;\<b-up-x\>|)>=<around*|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,<space|0.17em>B<around|(|v<rsub|1>|)>*<space|0.17em>e<rsup|i*<around|(|z<rsub|1>-z<rsub|2>|)>*H>*U*<around|(|\<b-up-x\><rsub|1>-\<b-up-x\><rsub|2>|)>*<space|0.17em>B<around|(|v<rsub|2>|)>*\<cdots\>*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>.
  </equation*>

  This function is holomorphic for <math|<math-up|Im><space|0.17em>z<rsub|1>\<less\>\<ldots\>\<less\><math-up|Im><space|0.17em>z<rsub|n>>,
  and it equals the Euclidean correlation at imaginary times. The Wightman
  theory is obtained in five steps:

  <\enumerate>
    <item><strong|Boundary values.> Bounds of order <math|k<rsup|-m>>, when
    all height gaps exceed <math|k>, give tempered boundary values
    <math|W<rsub|n>>. They converge against every joint Schwartz test as the
    vector of heights tends to zero through all ordered vectors.

    <item><strong|Positivity and fields.> This mode of convergence allows two
    independently regularized vectors to be compared. So <math|W> is a limit
    of Gram matrices of OS vectors: it is positive, and the Wightman space
    embeds isometrically into the OS space. Fields act on finite words modulo
    the null space by prepending a letter. This gives the invariant domain,
    the formal adjoints and the cyclic vacuum.

    <item><strong|Covariance and spectrum.> The rotation Ward identities of
    Part VI, in planes containing the time axis, continue to infinitesimal
    boost identities. These integrate to Lorentz covariance without any
    spectral assumption. The spectral measure of a Wightman vector equals
    that of its OS image, so it is supported in
    <math|p<rsup|0>\<geqslant\>0>; covariance then moves the support into the
    forward cone.

    <item><strong|Locality.> Only adjacent transpositions are needed. For a
    spacelike adjacent pair, both orderings have holomorphic germs,
    constructed with complex boosts. The germs agree to all orders on the
    Euclidean slice, because the Euclidean kernel is symmetric and smooth
    away from coincidences.

    <item><strong|Faithfulness.> A label whose Wightman functions vanish has
    a vanishing tube function, hence a vanishing Euclidean kernel.
  </enumerate>

  <strong|Every phase.> The low-energy bound uses only locality and the
  spectrum condition. Take <math|A> a finite sum of field words with
  compactly supported tests.

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>The commutator of time-translated copies of <math|A> vanishes for
    times at most <math|1> and spatial separations beyond a radius <math|R>
    independent of <math|\<xi\>>.

    <item>Average over unit time, pair with a spatial-momentum bump of radius
    <math|2*u> (whose Fourier transform is <math|O<around|(|u<rsup|3>|)>> in
    three space dimensions), and use <math|<around|\||\<b-up-p\>|\|>\<leqslant\>E>
    on the forward cone. This bounds the spectral weight of each shell
    <math|u/2\<less\>E\<leqslant\>u> by <math|O<around|(|u<rsup|2>|)>>, and
    dyadic summation gives the first bound below.
  </itemize-minus>

  <\equation*>
    \<mu\><rsub|A*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>>*<around|(|0\<less\>E\<leqslant\>u|)>\<leqslant\>M<rsub|A>*u<rsup|2>*<around|(|0\<less\>u\<leqslant\>1|)>,<space|2em>0\<leqslant\><around*|\<langle\>|A*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>,<around|(|e<rsup|-s*H<rsub|\<xi\>>>-P<rsub|0,\<xi\>>|)>*A*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>|\<rangle\>>\<leqslant\>M<rsub|A>*s<rsup|-2>*<around|(|s\<geqslant\>1|)>.
  </equation*>

  The constant <math|M<rsub|A>> is independent of <math|\<xi\>>, by
  compactness of <math|X>.

  Vacuum uniqueness then extends from almost every phase to every phase:

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>The function <math|\<xi\>\<mapsto\><around|\<langle\>|A*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>,e<rsup|-s*H<rsub|\<xi\>>>*A*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>|\<rangle\>>-<around|\||<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>,A*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>|\<rangle\>>|\|><rsup|2>>
    is continuous.

    <item>For fixed <math|A> and <math|s>, the set of phases where this
    function lies in <math|<around|[|0,M<rsub|A>*s<rsup|-2>|]>> is closed by
    continuity. It contains the full-measure set of phases with unique
    vacuum; full support of <math|\<nu\>> makes this closed set all of
    <math|X>.

    <item>Letting <math|s\<to\>\<infty\>> gives a unique vacuum in every
    phase.
  </itemize-minus>

  The comparison map from the Wightman space to the OS space is surjective,
  proved independently by pushing time-ordered vectors apart. This transfers
  vacuum uniqueness to the OS space. Each phase theory is irreducible. Its
  Wightman distributions are continuous in <math|\<xi\>>, and their
  <math|\<nu\>>-average is the boundary value of the continuation of
  <math|S<rsup|<math-up|mix>>>. The bounds allow spectrum down to zero and
  give only polynomial decay <math|s<rsup|-2>> in Euclidean time, not
  exponential clustering.

  <section|Part VI. Ward identities, rotations and the stress
  tensor><label|part-vi-ward-identities-rotations-and-the-stress-tensor>

  <em|Units: <hlink|VI.1 \V Ward identities, the rotation action and the
  conserved stress tensor|outline/units/VI-1.tm>; <hlink|VI.2 \V The free
  Maxwell stress tensor and the local Ward charge|outline/units/VI-2.tm>.>

  The lattice has only the hypercubic symmetries. The proof obtains the
  generators of rotations from stress fluxes, and the group action by
  comparison with free Maxwell theory. It uses the OS spaces of the mixed
  state in the coordinate directions and the free comparison of Part VII. It
  uses no decay of correlations.

  <strong|Free Maxwell theory.> Consider <math|dim \<frak-g\>> free Maxwell
  fields, with field spaces the <math|G>-invariant polynomials in on-shell
  curvature jets.

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>The centred, real, symmetric tensors of degree 4 that are covariant
    under signed permutations form a three-dimensional space.

    <item>For simple <math|\<frak-g\>>, the divergence maps this space onto a
    two-dimensional space. So the conserved tensors form a line, spanned by
    the Maxwell stress tensor.

    <item>For every Killing field <math|v>, the flux of
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|b>v<rsub|b>*T<rsub|a*b>> through a shell around an
    insertion equals the Lie derivative of the insertion. For elementary
    fields this follows from <math|-\<Delta\>*G<rsub|0>=\<delta\>>; for
    polynomials, from the Leibniz rule and Wick contraction.

    <item>An explicit three-point function fixes the sign and normalization
    of the Ward constant.
  </itemize-minus>

  <strong|Conserved tensor.> There are scaling sequences
  <math|r<rsub|k>*\<downarrow\>*0> with frames <math|U<rsub|k>>, isomorphisms
  for large <math|k>. Along them the rescaled interacting divergence
  converges to the free one.

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>Surjectivity is an open condition, so the interacting divergence is
    also onto, and its kernel is one-dimensional.

    <item>A nonzero conserved <math|J> in this kernel converges, after
    rescaling, to a multiple of the free stress tensor.
  </itemize-minus>

  <strong|Shell identities.> Lattice integration by parts in the link
  variables, with ultraviolet control of the error terms, gives shell
  identities. Take <math|\<chi\>> equal to <math|1> near <math|x<rsub|0>> and
  vanishing near the spectators:

  <\equation*>
    -<big|sum><rsub|a><big|int>\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<chi\><around|(|y|)>*<space|0.17em>S*<around*|(|J<rsub|a*b><around|(|y|)>*<space|0.17em>B<around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>*\<cdots\>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*y=S*<around*|(|<around|(|p<rsub|b>*B|)><around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>*\<cdots\>|)>.
  </equation*>

  With weight <math|K*<around|(|y-x<rsub|0>|)>>, for a rotation generator
  <math|K>, the same construction gives maps <math|Y<rsub|K>>. Here
  <math|p<rsub|b>> and <math|Y<rsub|K>> are linear maps on labels, not yet
  identified with derivatives and rotations. Moving the centre of the shell
  gives <math|\<partial\>*p=p*<space|0.17em>\<partial\>> and
  <math|\<partial\><rsub|b>*Y<rsub|K>+<big|sum><rsub|c>K<rsub|c*b>*<space|0.17em>p<rsub|c>=Y<rsub|K>*\<partial\><rsub|b>>.

  <strong|Flux at infinity.>

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>For rotations, cut off the rotation field so that it is compactly
    supported and equal to the exact field on a box far larger than the
    configuration.

    <item>Conservation and symmetry cancel the cross terms.

    <item>The remaining exterior contribution is bounded through spectral
    measures of the OS semigroups along and transverse to a coordinate line,
    and it tends to zero.

    <item>This gives the angular identity for configurations on one
    coordinate line, together with a line-sum identity, and hence
    transversality of the label maps.
  </itemize-minus>

  <strong|Normalization.> In free theory, the finitely many relations
  satisfied by <math|p> and <math|\<partial\>> at a given degree have a
  one-dimensional solution space. Injectivity is an open condition, so the
  same holds along the scaling sequence. Hence
  <math|p<rsub|b>=c*<space|0.17em>\<partial\><rsub|b>>, with <math|c>
  independent of the degree and <math|c\<ne\>0>. The tensor <math|T=J/c>
  satisfies

  <\equation*>
    -<big|sum><rsub|a><big|int>\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<chi\><around|(|y|)>*<space|0.17em>S<rsup|s>*<around*|(|T<rsub|a*b><around|(|y|)>*<space|0.17em>B<around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>*\<cdots\>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*y=<frac|\<partial\>|\<partial\>*x<rsub|0><rsup|b>>*<space|0.17em>S<rsup|s>*<around*|(|B<around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>*\<cdots\>|)>
  </equation*>

  in the mixed state. By the operator identities of Part V, it holds in every
  phase. Since <math|\<partial\>*\<chi\>> vanishes near every insertion, no
  contact terms arise.

  <strong|All separated configurations.> Positivity of Gram functions,
  together with the Ward values already obtained, gives analytic-vector
  bounds <math|<around|\<lVert\>|f<rsup|<around|(|m|)>>|\<rVert\>>\<leqslant\>C*<around|(|\<rho\>/2|)><rsup|-m>*m!>.
  The angular Ward functional is therefore real-analytic along walks that
  move one point at a time. Starting from configurations on a line, it
  vanishes on all configurations whose points have pairwise distinct first
  coordinates, and by continuity on all of <math|<math-up|Conf><rsub|n>>.

  <strong|Integration to <math|O<around|(|4|)>>.>

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>The bracket defect <math|<around|[|Y<rprime|'><rsub|u>,Y<rprime|'><rsub|v>|]>-Y<rprime|'><rsub|<around|[|u,v|]>>>
    (with <math|Y<rprime|'>=Y/c>) satisfies a summed identity. A rigidity
    statement, proved in free theory and transferred by the comparison,
    forces the defect to vanish.

    <item>The infinitesimal action converges to the Maxwell one:
    <math|U<rsub|k><rsup|-1>*Y<rprime|'><rsub|K>*U<rsub|k>\<to\><math-up|Lie><rsup|<math-up|Max>><rsub|K>>.

    <item>A perturbation of spectral projections of the Casimir operators
    gives exact intertwiners <math|V<rsub|d>>. The action on <math|F<rsub|d>>
    is <math|R<rsub|d><around|(|k|)>=V<rsub|d>*R<rsup|<math-up|Max>><rsub|d><around|(|k|)>*V<rsub|d><rsup|-1>>.

    <item>Since <math|t\<mapsto\>S<around|(|R<around|(|e<rsup|t*K>|)>*v;e<rsup|t*K>*x|)>>
    has zero derivative, and reflections act through the real structure,
    <math|S<around|(|R<around|(|k|)>*v;k*x|)>=S<around|(|v;x|)>> for all
    <math|k\<in\>O<around|(|4|)>>.

    <item>Averaging <math|J/c> over <math|O<around|(|4|)>> gives a conserved,
    real, symmetric, covariant tensor that satisfies both the translation and
    the rotation Ward identities.
  </itemize-minus>

  <section|Part VII. Identification as Yang\UMills
  theory><label|part-vii-identification-as-yangmills-theory>

  <subsection|Free Maxwell fields at short
  distance><label|free-maxwell-fields-at-short-distance>

  <em|Units: <hlink|VII.1 \V Free Maxwell comparison
  theory|outline/units/VII-1.tm>; <hlink|VI.2 \V Interacting stress
  comparison|outline/units/VI-2.tm>; <hlink|VII.6 \V Scaling variants and
  fixed-field estimates|outline/units/VII-6.tm>. The comparison is assembled
  in <hlink|VIII.1|outline/units/VIII-1.tm>.>

  <strong|The reference theory.> It consists of <math|dim \<frak-g\>> free
  Maxwell fields built from <math|\<Gamma\><around|(|x|)>=1/<around|(|4*\<pi\><rsup|2><around|\||x|\|><rsup|2>|)>>.

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>On the lattice, corrected covariances of finite-difference jets
    converge to <math|\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<partial\><rsub|b>*\<Gamma\>> away
    from coincidences.

    <item>Wick moments satisfy the same Gaussian recursion on the lattice and
    in the continuum, so correlations of quotient labels converge.

    <item>A triangular basis built from the Kelvin transform gives strict
    reflected Gram positivity for every label outside the ideal
    <math|I<rsub|d>> generated by the free Maxwell equations and the Bianchi
    identities.

    <item>So the free null space is exactly <math|I<rsub|d>>, and the free
    field space <math|M<rsub|d>=P<rsub|d>/I<rsub|d>>, where <math|P<rsub|d>>
    is the corresponding space of polynomials in free curvature jets, is
    detected by finitely many correlations.
  </itemize-minus>

  <strong|The comparison.> There are scales <math|r<rsub|k>*\<downarrow\>*0>
  and linear frames <math|U<rsub|k>> on the field spaces with the following
  property. The correlations of the mixed state, rescaled by <math|r<rsub|k>>
  and transformed by <math|U<rsub|k>>, converge to the free Maxwell
  correlations:

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>with all derivatives;

    <item>uniformly on compact subsets of <math|<math-up|Conf><rsub|n>>;

    <item>for all degrees along one sequence.
  </itemize-minus>

  The stress tensor converges to the Maxwell stress tensor. An upper bound on
  <math|dim F<rsub|d>> makes the frames isomorphisms for large <math|k>, so
  <math|F<rsub|d>\<cong\>M<rsub|d>>.

  The frames are necessary: the claim is not that a fixed normalization of
  each composite field has a limit along every sequence of radii. The
  schedule contains a real parameter that moves the scale at which
  observables are read, and the estimates are uniform in it. Tuning this
  parameter, every sequence of radii tending to zero has a subsequence along
  which the comparison holds.

  <subsection|Running coupling><label|running-coupling>

  <em|Unit: <hlink|VII.5 \V Asymptotic freedom of the running
  coupling|outline/units/VII-5.tm>.>

  With <math|g<rsub|N,p><around|(|m|)>=\<beta\><rsub|N,p,m><rsup|-1/2>>, Part
  III gives

  <\equation*>
    g<rsub|N,p><around|(|m|)><space|0.17em><sqrt|log
    <around|(|1/a<rsub|m>|)>>\<longrightarrow\><frac|1|<sqrt|2*b<rsub|0>>>*<space|2em><around|(|m\<to\>\<infty\>|)>,
  </equation*>

  uniformly in <math|N\<geqslant\>m> and <math|p>. This is the
  leading-logarithmic running of the coupling of the construction's effective
  actions. No two-loop term, no <math|\<Lambda\>> parameter and no comparison
  with another renormalization scheme is claimed. For
  <math|S*U<around|(|N|)>>, the conventionally normalized coupling
  <math|g<rsub|<math-up|std>>=<sqrt|2*N>*<space|0.17em>g> satisfies
  <math|g<rsub|<math-up|std>>*<sqrt|log <around|(|1/a<rsub|m>|)>>\<to\><around|(|11*N/24*\<pi\><rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1/2>>.

  <subsection|The trace law and nonfreeness><label|the-trace-law-and-nonfreeness>

  <em|Units: <hlink|VII.2 \V The trace of the stress tensor and the trace
  anomaly|outline/units/VII-2.tm>; <hlink|VII.3 \V The theory is not free:
  Gram positivity and exclusion of free-field
  profiles|outline/units/VII-3.tm>.>

  <strong|Leading form of the trace.> The classical Maxwell and Yang\UMills
  stress tensors are traceless. The quantum trace comes from the scale
  dependence of the coefficient of curvature squared.

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>The proof pulls the trace of the lattice stress observable back
    through the RG steps.

    <item>Fourth-order matching identifies the leading part of the trace as
    the derivative of the effective action with respect to scale.

    <item>The one-loop coefficient enters through a momentum-shell estimate
    of the kind used in Part III.
  </itemize-minus>

  Along the scaling sequences, with <math|\<beta\><rsub|k>> the running
  coupling at the RG level that reads scale <math|r<rsub|k>>, and
  <math|O<rsub|F<rsup|2>>> the normalized curvature-squared label,

  <\equation*>
    \<beta\><rsub|k>*<space|0.17em>r<rsub|k><rsup|4>*<space|0.17em>U<rsub|k><rsup|-1>*\<Theta\>\<longrightarrow\>4*d<rsub|2>*<space|0.17em>O<rsub|F<rsup|2>>,<space|2em>d<rsub|2>=-<frac|\<kappa\><rsub|G>|4>,<space|2em><frac|log
    <around|(|1/r<rsub|k>|)>|\<beta\><rsub|k>>\<longrightarrow\><frac|1|\<kappa\><rsub|G>>.
  </equation*>

  In perturbative terms, this is the familiar
  <math|\<Theta\>\<simeq\><frac|\<beta\><around|(|g|)>|2*g<rsup|3>>*F<rsup|2>>,
  with <math|\<beta\><around|(|g|)>/<around|(|2*g<rsup|3>|)>=-b<rsub|0>/2=d<rsub|2>>
  at one loop. The trace is a curvature-squared field suppressed by
  <math|g<rsup|2><around|(|r|)>\<simeq\>1/<around|(|\<kappa\><rsub|G>*log
  <around|(|1/r|)>|)>>.

  <strong|The two-point law.> Here <math|\<Theta\>=<big|sum><rsub|\<mu\>>T<rsub|\<mu\>*\<mu\>>-S<rsup|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|\<mu\>>T<rsub|\<mu\>*\<mu\>>|)>*\<b-1\>>,
  in the selected phase, which has a unique vacuum.

  <\enumerate>
    <item>The free two-point function of <math|O<rsub|F<rsup|2>>> is
    <math|3*dim \<frak-g\>/<around|(|\<pi\><rsup|4><around|\||x|\|><rsup|8>|)>>.

    <item>So at lattice-fitted configurations along the scaling sequence,
    <math|r<rsub|k><rsup|8>*log<rsup|2><around|(|1/r<rsub|k>|)>*<around|\<langle\>|\<Theta\>*\<Theta\>|\<rangle\>>\<to\><around|(|4*d<rsub|2>/\<kappa\><rsub|G>|)><rsup|2>\<cdot\>3*dim
    \<frak-g\>/\<pi\><rsup|4>=3*dim \<frak-g\>/\<pi\><rsup|4>>.

    <item>Rotation invariance moves the pair onto the time axis.

    <item>The spectral representation in the phase gives
    <math|G<rsub|\<Theta\>><around|(|r|)>=<big|int>e<rsup|-r*E>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><around|(|E|)>>
    with a positive measure. So <math|G<rsub|\<Theta\>>> is nonincreasing,
    which sandwiches <math|G<rsub|\<Theta\>><around|(|r|)>> between its
    values at fitted points.

    <item>If the limit failed along some sequence of radii, the flexible
    scaling above would give a subsequence along which it holds, a
    contradiction.
  </enumerate>

  Hence the limit exists over all radii:

  <\equation*>
    lim<rsub|r*\<downarrow\>*0> <space|0.17em>r<rsup|8>*log<rsup|2><around|(|1/r|)>*<space|0.17em>G<rsub|\<Theta\>><around|(|r|)>=<frac|3*dim<rsub|\<bbb-R\>>
    \<frak-g\>|\<pi\><rsup|4>>.
  </equation*>

  The amplitude does not depend on the value of the one-loop coefficient.
  <math|\<kappa\><rsub|G>> enters once through the trace and once through the
  running, and <math|<around|(|4*d<rsub|2>/\<kappa\><rsub|G>|)><rsup|2>=1>
  identically. So the test detects the inverse-logarithmic suppression and
  the free normalization of <math|F<rsup|2>>, not the value of
  <math|b<rsub|0>>; that value is established by the running law of Part III.

  <strong|Exclusion of free profiles.> Consider a finite Wick profile
  <math|W<around|(|r|)>=<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>>c<rsub|\<alpha\>>*<big|prod><rsub|\<beta\>>K<rsub|\<alpha\>*\<beta\>><around|(|r|)>>.
  Each factor <math|K<around|(|r|)>=<big|int>E<rsup|q>*<space|0.17em>h<around|(|\<b-up-p\>|)>*<around|(|2*E|)><rsup|-1>*e<rsup|-r*E>*<space|0.17em>d<rsup|3>*\<b-up-p\>>
  has <math|E=<sqrt|<math-ss|m><rsup|2>+<around|\||\<b-up-p\>|\|><rsup|2>>>
  and <math|h> homogeneous.

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item><math|W> has an asymptotic expansion at <math|r=0> in the functions
    <math|r<rsup|a><around|(|log r|)><rsup|k>>, with <math|a\<in\>\<bbb-Z\>>
    and <math|k\<geqslant\>0>.

    <item>Along <math|r=2<rsup|-n>>, <math|r<rsup|8>*log<rsup|2><around|(|1/r|)>*<space|0.17em>W<around|(|r|)>>
    is then a finite sum of terms <math|c*<space|0.17em>\<rho\><rsup|n>*n<rsup|k+2>>,
    which can converge only to <math|0>.

    <item>Since the limit for <math|G<rsub|\<Theta\>>> is positive,
    <math|G<rsub|\<Theta\>>> differs from <math|W> at points arbitrarily
    close to <math|0>.
  </itemize-minus>

  This excludes exactly the finite class of profiles in the statement, and it
  concerns only <math|G<rsub|\<Theta\>>> on the time axis.

  The stronger statement adds further tests:

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>a Wightman-side Wick test;

    <item>Maxwell\UFock tests. Consider any isometric embedding of the
    selected phase's Hilbert space into a Fock space of transverse photons
    that preserves the vacuum and translations and has transverse symmetric
    image. No such embedding makes <math|\<Theta\>> local with respect to the
    Weyl operators.
  </itemize-minus>

  <subsection|Operator product expansion and contact
  extensions><label|operator-product-expansion-and-contact-extensions>

  <em|Unit: <hlink|VII.4 \V Operator product expansion and contact
  extensions|outline/units/VII-4.tm>.>

  <strong|The lattice statement.> The product of insertions in one cell
  equals a single insertion of the same total degree, plus a remainder with
  small chessboard root. The proof is an induction over the RG levels between
  the cluster scale and the observation scale: the part of degree at most
  <math|D> is kept, and the rest is paid into the remainder.

  <strong|The continuum coefficients.> They are read off rather than given by
  a formula.

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>A prescription fixes, degree by degree, biorthogonal systems of
    fields and detectors. The detectors are finite combinations of
    mixed-state correlations of the cluster with fixed, separated exterior
    words.

    <item>The prescription is averaged over <math|O<around|(|4|)>> and
    symmetrized for reality.

    <item>The coefficient <math|c<rsub|j,\<kappa\>><around|(|a;x,o|)>> is a
    coordinate of the cluster's new component of degree <math|j> in a real
    basis.
  </itemize-minus>

  Consider an <math|<around|(|r,M,\<eta\>|)>>-cluster: points within
  <math|M*r> of <math|o> and at mutual distance at least <math|\<eta\>*r>,
  carrying fields of total degree <math|w>. Take spectators at distance at
  least <math|c> from <math|o> and from each other. Then, for all
  sufficiently small <math|r>,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||c<rsub|j,\<kappa\>><around|(|a;x,o|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>r<rsup|<space|0.17em>j-w-\<varepsilon\>>,<space|2em><mid|\|>S<rsup|s><around|(|a,x;b,y|)>-<big|sum><rsub|j\<leqslant\>D><big|sum><rsub|\<kappa\>>c<rsub|j,\<kappa\>><around|(|a;x,o|)>*<space|0.17em>S<rsup|s><around|(|O<rsub|j,\<kappa\>>,o;b,y|)><mid|\|>\<leqslant\>r<rsup|<space|0.17em>D+1-w-\<varepsilon\>>.
  </equation*>

  <strong|Uniformity and limits of the statement.>

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>The coefficients do not depend on the spectators <math|b> and
    <math|y>, on the state <math|s>, or on <math|D>.

    <item>The threshold for <math|r> depends on the fields, on
    <math|M,\<eta\>,c,\<varepsilon\>,D>, and in the first bound on the
    channel, but it is uniform in <math|s>, <math|x>, <math|o> and <math|y>.

    <item>Uniformity in the spectators comes from the train argument of Part
    V, in which the exterior word is paid once.

    <item>Uniformity in the state comes from bounding the error in the
    zero-energy algebra, whose norm dominates every character and the
    mixture.

    <item>The loss <math|\<varepsilon\>> absorbs logarithms. There is no
    logarithmic refinement, so the bound is weaker than the perturbative OPE.

    <item>Uniqueness of the coefficients is not asserted. Neither convergence
    as <math|D\<to\>\<infty\>> nor agreement with perturbation theory is
    claimed.

    <item>Nested versions over finite trees of clusters, and integrated
    versions against measures of bounded variation, also hold.
  </itemize-minus>

  <strong|Contact extensions.> These use Taylor subtractions over forests of
  collapsing clusters on dyadic shells. The OPE remainder makes the shell
  sums converge. For every state and every list of labels this gives a
  distribution of finite order on <math|<around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|4>|)><rsup|n>>
  that agrees with the correlation on <math|<math-up|Conf><rsub|n>>. Not
  established: uniqueness, reflection positivity on overlapping supports,
  contact Ward identities, real-time products, and convergence of the lattice
  correlations on the diagonals.

  <section|Part VIII. Assembly><label|part-viii-assembly>

  <em|Unit: <hlink|VIII.1 \V Assembly of the main
  theorem|outline/units/VIII-1.tm>.>

  The construction run depends only on <math|G> and the penalty <math|b>. It
  is chosen before any extraction order or observable. For each extraction
  order (a spacing sequence and a volume sequence), the assembly composes
  further refinements:

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>one refinement for the limits of the running coupling;

    <item>one that produces the limit table, with the Euclidean hierarchy,
    stress tensor, phases and reconstruction of Parts IV\UVI.
  </itemize-minus>

  To these it adds the reconstructions of all phases, the OPE prescription,
  the nested and contact results and the Maxwell comparison. Everything is
  transported to a common refinement, so all objects refer to one table.

  The public theorem is the case <math|b=1>, with the full spacing sequence
  and the given volume sequence; its <math|q> is the volume part of the
  refinement.

  No new estimate enters, but identifying all objects with one table is a
  real constraint. Separate existence results for a lattice limit, a Wightman
  theory, an OPE and a trace law would not show that they concern the same
  correlation functions. The formal construction supplies the hypotheses
  under which the limit theory is first proved. The outlines have not
  explained every supplier in full; the <hlink|assembly
  unit|outline/units/VIII-1.tm#7-what-remains-unexplained> records the limits
  of its source reading.

  <section|Relation to earlier work, and where the difficulty
  lies><label|relation-to-earlier-work-and-where-the-difficulty-lies>

  <strong|Classical sources.>

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>Balaban's program is the model for the ultraviolet analysis:
    block-spin transformations, gauge fixing around a background field,
    fluctuation covariances built from averaging operators, the
    small-field/large-field decomposition, and renormalization of the
    marginal coupling.

    <item>The classical cluster expansion (Glimm\UJaffe, Brydges,
    Kotecký\UPreiss) organizes the polymer activities.

    <item>Reflection positivity (Osterwalder\USeiler) and chessboard
    estimates (Glimm\UJaffe\USpencer, Fröhlich\UIsrael\ULieb\USimon) connect
    lattice estimates with bounds on observables.

    <item>Osterwalder\USchrader and Glimm\UJaffe reconstruction turns
    Euclidean correlations into Wightman fields.

    <item>The slope is the one-loop coefficient of Gross\UWilczek and
    Politzer.
  </itemize-minus>

  <strong|Departures from Balaban's scheme.>

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>The block kernel is an exactly normalized, gauge-covariant Markov
    kernel with random tags, not a linear averaging operator in a fixed
    background gauge.

    <item>Integration uses a spanning-forest gauge.

    <item>The block ratios grow along the levels.

    <item>The threshold carries the factor <math|e<rsup|<around|(|log log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^>|)><rsup|2>>>.

    <item>The construction includes an analytic family in a tuning parameter.
    The unit outlines do not establish a comprehensive comparison with
    Balaban's papers.
  </itemize-minus>

  <strong|Beyond the ultraviolet analysis>, the proof does the following:

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>One schedule and one family serve composite fields of every degree.
    Each field has a fixed normalization, root bounds uniform in the volume,
    and derivatives controlled by rank detection, with no coupling-dependent
    loss per site.

    <item>Convergence at fixed volume is proved despite tuning that depends
    on the cutoff.

    <item>Equicontinuity in infinite volume comes from a rank-free trace
    inequality, without derivative bounds.

    <item>Commutativity of the zero-energy algebra is proved, not assumed.

    <item>Vacuum uniqueness in every phase follows from a uniform low-energy
    bound that replaces a mass gap or a cluster argument.

    <item>Lorentz covariance comes from Ward identities.

    <item>Rotation invariance is restored without decay of correlations, and
    the group action is obtained by comparison with free Maxwell theory.

    <item>The trace law holds as a limit over all radii.

    <item>The OPE is derived from the RG, with one system of coefficients for
    the mixture and every phase.
  </itemize-minus>

  <strong|Where the difficulty lies.>

  <\enumerate>
    <item><strong|Gauge degeneracy and large fields> must be controlled with
    a bounded cost per site and per seed. This is done by the gauge count of
    the determinant budget, the energy lower bound for seeds, and the
    exactness of the kernel.

    <item><strong|Effective-action estimates must become estimates for
    observables>: for every composite field, its derivatives and its
    fixed-volume limit, inside one family.

    <item><strong|The passage from a lattice mixture to relativistic phases>
    has no continuous symmetry and no clustering to start from. It is handled
    by Ward identities with free comparison, the commutative zero-energy
    algebra, and spectral bounds based on locality.
  </enumerate>

  The order of choice of constants and the transport of estimates through the
  construction account for a substantial part of the formal development. Its
  length alone does not measure mathematical novelty.

  <section|Steps whose informal account is
  incomplete><label|steps-whose-informal-account-is-incomplete>

  The theorem is proved in Lean, and the conditional statements of the limit
  theory are discharged within its proof. The unit outlines, however,
  describe the following steps only at the level of statements, or with
  readings marked tentative. A written proof will have to supply them.

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item><strong|One-loop identification (III.7, VII.2).> The slope bound is
    first proved for an auxiliary functional and then transferred to the
    completed Hessian of the actual step. The revised
    <hlink|III.7|outline/units/III-7.tm#7-what-remains-unexplained> explains
    the determinant factorization and the transfer of the coefficient;
    several subsidiary real-quotient, complex-strip, tadpole, bubble and
    alias estimates remain inputs at statement level. The trace amplitude
    does not test this value.

    <item><strong|Covariance and Hessian (III.4).> The Gaussian covariance is
    the inverse of the gauge-fixed Gram form on the active links. Whether it
    equals the inverse Hessian of the effective action at the background is
    left open.

    <item><strong|Nonlinear step.>

    <\itemize-minus>
      <item>The correspondence between the outputs of the one-step bounds
      (III.10) and the norms restored by the reset (III.9) is not written
      out.

      <item>Several hypotheses of the step were read only as statements.

      <item>The cluster expansion is proved for real weights (III.5), while
      the fixed-volume argument uses complex tuning.
    </itemize-minus>

    <item><strong|Fixed-volume convergence (III.12, IV.1).>
    <hlink|III.12|outline/units/III-12.tm#7-what-remains-unexplained> now
    explains fixed-level convergence, the uniform tail bound and the Cauchy
    argument; the underlying trajectory-convergence and multiscale estimates
    remain imported inputs. <hlink|IV.1|outline/units/IV-1.tm> locates the
    upgrade to the full spacing sequence, while leaving the upstream
    construction of its fixed-volume kernels outside that unit's review.

    <item><strong|Shell identities and the flux at infinity (VI.1, VI.2).>
    The lattice derivation of the shell identities is given only at statement
    level. The exterior-flux estimate was not read. The descent of the
    rotation action to exact intertwiners is marked tentative.

    <item><strong|Reconstruction (V.2).> The boundary values, the spectrum
    condition and the locality argument are described from statements; their
    mechanisms were confirmed in the Lean but not written out in the unit
    outlines.

    <item><strong|Contact extensions and nested OPE (VII.4).> The shell
    estimates and the nested rates are described from docstrings.

    <item><strong|Field relations (II.1).> It is not shown directly that the
    lattice equation relations exhaust the null space. The identification
    <math|F<rsub|d>\<cong\>M<rsub|d>> comes from the comparison with free
    theory.
  </itemize-minus>

  <section|Reading guide><label|reading-guide>

  The <hlink|exact statement|statement.tm> separates the public theorem from
  the stronger construction. The <hlink|architecture|outline/index.tm>
  describes what each part hands to the next. For the stated inputs and
  remaining gaps in this account, follow the relevant unit's \PWhat it uses\Q
  and \PWhat remains unexplained\Q sections. The <hlink|assembly
  unit|outline/units/VIII-1.tm> describes how the construction is put
  together.

  <block|<tformat|<twith|table-width|1par>|<twith|table-hmode|exact>|<cwith|1|-1|1|-1|cell-hyphen|t>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-valign|bottom>|<table|<row|<cell|<strong|Question>>|<cell|<strong|Units
  to read>>>|<row|<cell|What are the fields, and how are their normalizations
  fixed?>|<cell|<hlink|II.1|outline/units/II-1.tm>,
  <hlink|III.12|outline/units/III-12.tm>, <hlink|IV.1|outline/units/IV-1.tm>,
  <hlink|VII.1|outline/units/VII-1.tm>>>|<row|<cell|How does the ultraviolet
  analysis relate to Balaban's?>|<cell|<hlink|III.1|outline/units/III-1.tm>,
  <hlink|III.3|outline/units/III-3.tm>, <hlink|III.4|outline/units/III-4.tm>,
  <hlink|III.6|outline/units/III-6.tm>, <hlink|III.5|outline/units/III-5.tm>,
